1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,330
Section 3

2
00:00:01,330 --> 00:00:02,215
TUTOR:

3
00:00:02,215 --> 00:00:02,878
OK, Jim.

4
00:00:02,878 --> 00:00:07,220
You wanted to see me about your textile design project.

5
00:00:07,220 --> 00:00:07,270
JIM:

6
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:08,265
That's right.

7
00:00:08,265 --> 00:00:14,533
I've been looking at how a range of natural dyes can be used to colour fabrics like cotton

8
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:15,230
and wool.

9
00:00:15,230 --> 00:00:15,620
TUTOR:

10
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,960
Why did you choose that topic?

11
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:18,283
JIM:

12
00:00:18,283 --> 00:00:22,801
Well, I got a lot of useful ideas from the museum, you know, at

13
00:00:22,801 --> 00:00:24,092
that exhibition of textiles.

14
00:00:24,092 --> 00:00:27,642
But I've always been interested in anything to do with colour.

15
00:00:27,642 --> 00:00:32,160
Years ago, I went to a carpet shop with my parents when we were

16
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:33,451
on holiday in Turkey,

17
00:00:33,451 --> 00:00:35,710
and I remember all the amazing colours.

18
00:00:35,710 --> 00:00:36,156
TUTOR:

19
00:00:36,156 --> 00:00:39,720
They might not all have been natural dyes.

20
00:00:39,720 --> 00:00:40,083
JIM:

21
00:00:40,083 --> 00:00:40,809
Maybe not,

22
00:00:40,809 --> 00:00:44,441
but for the project I decided to follow it up.

23
00:00:44,441 --> 00:00:49,524
And I found a great book about a botanic garden in California that specialises

24
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:51,340
in plants used for dyes.

25
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:51,828
TUTOR:

26
00:00:51,828 --> 00:00:52,317
OK.

27
00:00:52,317 --> 00:00:57,690
So, in your project, you had to include a practical investigation.

28
00:00:57,690 --> 00:00:58,066
JIM:

29
00:00:58,066 --> 00:00:58,442
Yeah.

30
00:00:58,442 --> 00:01:01,450
At first I couldn't decide on my variables.

31
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:06,714
I was going to just look at one type of fibre for example, like

32
00:01:06,714 --> 00:01:07,090
cotton ...

33
00:01:07,090 --> 00:01:07,475
TUTOR:

34
00:01:07,475 --> 00:01:07,525
...

35
00:01:07,525 --> 00:01:10,940
and see how different types of dyes affected it?

36
00:01:10,940 --> 00:01:11,341
JIM:

37
00:01:11,341 --> 00:01:11,741
Yes.

38
00:01:11,741 --> 00:01:14,945
Then I decided to include others as well,

39
00:01:14,945 --> 00:01:18,550
so I looked at cotton and wool and nylon.

40
00:01:18,550 --> 00:01:18,936
TUTOR:

41
00:01:18,936 --> 00:01:21,250
With just one type of dye?

42
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:21,734
JIM:

43
00:01:21,734 --> 00:01:26,090
Various types, including some that weren't natural, for comparison.

44
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:26,895
TUTOR:

45
00:01:26,895 --> 00:01:27,700
OK.

46
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:28,071
JIM:

47
00:01:28,071 --> 00:01:30,664
So, I did the experiments last week.

48
00:01:30,664 --> 00:01:35,852
I used some ready-made natural dyes, I found a website which supplied them, they

49
00:01:35,852 --> 00:01:38,076
came in just a few days,

50
00:01:38,076 --> 00:01:41,040
but I also made some of my own.

51
00:01:41,040 --> 00:01:41,318
TUTOR:

52
00:01:41,318 --> 00:01:43,820
That must have taken quite a bit of time.

53
00:01:43,820 --> 00:01:44,136
JIM:

54
00:01:44,136 --> 00:01:47,934
Yes, I'd thought it'd just be a matter of a teaspoon or

55
00:01:47,934 --> 00:01:48,884
so of dye,

56
00:01:48,884 --> 00:01:51,416
and actually that wasn't the case at all.

57
00:01:51,416 --> 00:01:54,897
Like I was using one vegetable, beetroot, for a red dye,

58
00:01:54,897 --> 00:01:58,378
and I had to chop up a whole pile of it.

59
00:01:58,378 --> 00:02:00,910
So it all took longer than I'd expected.

60
00:02:00,910 --> 00:02:01,375
TUTOR:

61
00:02:01,375 --> 00:02:04,630
One possibility is to use food colourings.

62
00:02:04,630 --> 00:02:04,968
JIM:

63
00:02:04,968 --> 00:02:06,322
I did use one.

64
00:02:06,322 --> 00:02:09,030
That was a yellow dye, an artificial one.

65
00:02:09,030 --> 00:02:09,995
TUTOR:

66
00:02:09,995 --> 00:02:10,960
Tartrazine?

67
00:02:10,960 --> 00:02:11,260
JIM:

68
00:02:11,260 --> 00:02:11,561
Yeah.

69
00:02:11,561 --> 00:02:13,362
I used it on cotton first.

70
00:02:13,362 --> 00:02:15,164
It came out a great colour,

71
00:02:15,164 --> 00:02:18,466
but when I rinsed the material, the colour just washed away.

72
00:02:18,466 --> 00:02:21,169
I'd been going to try it out on nylon,

73
00:02:21,169 --> 00:02:22,670
but I abandoned that idea.

74
00:02:22,670 --> 00:02:23,143
TUTOR:

75
00:02:23,143 --> 00:02:25,980
Were you worried about health issues?

76
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:26,282
JIM:

77
00:02:26,282 --> 00:02:29,900
I'd thought if it's a legal food colouring, it must be safe.

78
00:02:29,900 --> 00:02:30,354
TUTOR:

79
00:02:30,354 --> 00:02:34,440
Well, it can occasionally cause allergic reactions, I believe.

80
00:02:34,440 --> 00:02:34,803
TUTOR:

81
00:02:34,803 --> 00:02:37,710
So what natural dyes did you look at?

82
00:02:37,710 --> 00:02:38,156
JIM:

83
00:02:38,156 --> 00:02:39,940
Well, one was turmeric.

84
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:43,508
The colour's great, it's a really strong yellow.

85
00:02:43,508 --> 00:02:46,630
It's generally used in dishes like curry.

86
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:46,912
TUTOR:

87
00:02:46,912 --> 00:02:50,013
It's meant to be quite good for your health when eaten,

88
00:02:50,013 --> 00:02:53,678
but you might find it's not permanent when it's used as a dye -

89
00:02:53,678 --> 00:02:54,524
a few washes,

90
00:02:54,524 --> 00:02:55,370
and it's gone.

91
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:55,728
JIM:

92
00:02:55,728 --> 00:02:56,086
Right.

93
00:02:56,086 --> 00:02:58,952
I used beetroot as a dye for wool.

94
00:02:58,952 --> 00:03:03,966
When I chop up beetroot to eat I always end up with bright red

95
00:03:03,966 --> 00:03:04,324
hands,

96
00:03:04,324 --> 00:03:08,622
but the wool ended up just a sort of watery cream shade.

97
00:03:08,622 --> 00:03:08,980
Disappointing.

98
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:09,308
TUTOR:

99
00:03:09,308 --> 00:03:11,602
There's a natural dye called Tyrian purple.

100
00:03:11,602 --> 00:03:13,240
Have you heard of that?

101
00:03:13,240 --> 00:03:13,601
JIM:

102
00:03:13,601 --> 00:03:13,962
Yes.

103
00:03:13,962 --> 00:03:15,768
It comes from a shellfish,

104
00:03:15,768 --> 00:03:21,184
and it was worn in ancient times but only by important people as it was

105
00:03:21,184 --> 00:03:21,906
so rare.

106
00:03:21,906 --> 00:03:23,350
I didn't use it.

107
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:23,678
TUTOR:

108
00:03:23,678 --> 00:03:28,267
It fell out of use centuries ago, though one researcher managed to get hold

109
00:03:28,267 --> 00:03:29,250
of some recently.

110
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:33,183
But that shade of purple can be produced by chemical dyes nowadays.

111
00:03:33,183 --> 00:03:35,150
Did you use any black dyes?

112
00:03:35,150 --> 00:03:35,545
JIM:

113
00:03:35,545 --> 00:03:35,939
Logwood.

114
00:03:35,939 --> 00:03:37,518
That was quite complicated.

115
00:03:37,518 --> 00:03:41,860
I had to prepare the fabric so the dye would take.

116
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:42,169
TUTOR:

117
00:03:42,169 --> 00:03:44,640
I hope you were careful to wear gloves.

118
00:03:44,640 --> 00:03:44,961
JIM:

119
00:03:44,961 --> 00:03:45,282
Yes.

120
00:03:45,282 --> 00:03:47,530
I know the danger with that dye.

121
00:03:47,530 --> 00:03:47,902
TUTOR:

122
00:03:47,902 --> 00:03:48,275
Good.

123
00:03:48,275 --> 00:03:51,255
It can be extremely dangerous if it's ingested.

124
00:03:51,255 --> 00:03:54,980
Now, presumably you had a look at an insect-based dye?

125
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:56,470
Like cochineal, for example?

126
00:03:56,470 --> 00:03:56,803
JIM:

127
00:03:56,803 --> 00:03:57,136
Yes.

128
00:03:57,136 --> 00:04:03,458
I didn't actually make that, I didn't have time to start crushing up insects to get the red colour

129
00:04:03,458 --> 00:04:05,454
and anyway they're not available here,

130
00:04:05,454 --> 00:04:09,448
but I managed to get the dye quite easily from a website.

131
00:04:09,448 --> 00:04:11,111
But it cost a fortune.

132
00:04:11,111 --> 00:04:14,439
I can see why it's generally just used in cooking,

133
00:04:14,439 --> 00:04:15,770
and in small quantities.

134
00:04:15,770 --> 00:04:16,125
TUTOR:

135
00:04:16,125 --> 00:04:17,547
Yes, it's very effective,

136
00:04:17,547 --> 00:04:21,100
but that's precisely why it's not used as a dye.

137
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:21,475
JIM:

138
00:04:21,475 --> 00:04:24,103
I also read about using metal oxide.

139
00:04:24,103 --> 00:04:29,358
Apparently you can allow iron to rust while it's in contact with the fabric,

140
00:04:29,358 --> 00:04:30,860
and that colours it.

141
00:04:30,860 --> 00:04:31,156
TUTOR:

142
00:04:31,156 --> 00:04:31,452
Yes,

143
00:04:31,452 --> 00:04:33,229
that works well for dying cotton.

144
00:04:33,229 --> 00:04:37,373
But you have to be careful as the metal can actually affect the fabric

145
00:04:37,373 --> 00:04:41,518
and so you can't expect to get a lot of wear out of fabrics

146
00:04:41,518 --> 00:04:42,703
treated in this way.

147
00:04:42,703 --> 00:04:45,663
And the colours are quite subtle, not everyone likes them.

148
00:04:45,663 --> 00:04:48,920
Anyway, it looks as if you've done a lot of work ...
